  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 220 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nThere remains the potential for wrap around precipitation across the central \nMtns into the Pikes Peak region and southeast Mtns into Thursday, with gusty \nnortherly winds of 20 to 40 mph developing across the plains through the day.\nTemperatures warm slightly for Thursday, with highs in the 50s to around 60 \nacross the plains, and mainly in the 40s and 50s across the higher terrain, \nsave for 30s at the peaks. With drier air working into the region and \nweakening gradient winds, overnight lows in the 20s are expected across\nthe higher terrain, with lows in the 30s across the plains, leading to \nthe potential for frost and the first freeze of the season across southeast\nColorado. We will continue to monitor for potential freeze highlights later\nThursday night, however, those needing to protect sensitive plants and \noutdoor irrigation should start to take action for the potential end of the \ngrowing season across the plains.  \n\nModel data continues to indicate warmer and drier conditions developing \nfor Friday and into the weekend, as upper level ridging builds into the \nregion from the west. This gives way to increasing southwest flow aloft \nonce again into early next week, ahead of another system moving across\nthe Great Basin bringing increasing chances of precipitation to areas\nalong and west of the ContDvd through the middle of next week.",
